<h2>How we worked</h2>
<ul class="points">
  <li><b>Around Pierre's code.</b> Pierre looked after Feedra's code, and once he'd walked us through how its audio engine handles fades, loops and playback, our designs got much more precise: a clear choice of whether a fade begins or finishes at its marker, a small graph beside each crossfade curve, and one place to choose concurrent, shuffled or sequential playback.</li>
  <li><b>Every mode made useful.</b> Loops, one-shots, shuffling and random delays can combine into a mess, so we tried the combinations by ear with Pierre and turned the ones that worked into presets.</li>
  <li><b>Tested on people.</b> First-click tests and surveys showed people missing a pad's edit button and mixing up mute and delete, so the edit button got bigger and a label, mute became a speaker that changes colour, and delete moved away, with a confirmation. The website's feature names got plainer, too: "3D audio positioning", not "spatialisation".</li>
  <li><b>A setback.</b> Midway through, the drive holding the website (and, unwisely, its backup) failed, so I rebuilt the site in Wix Studio, with everything backed up in the cloud from then on.</li>
  <li><b>Agile.</b> A Kanban board in Notion, shared with Pierre, half-week sprints and a short retrospective every week.</li>
</ul>

